  "notes": "Drawn from one photo of N37504 (737-9 MAX, Dec 2025) taken from below and ahead, so positions are approximate; the line's path is taken to match the 2019 scheme's (which kept it, in navy), and the globe's place is the same. Being replaced by the 2019 livery as aircraft are repainted. Re-checked 2026-11-15 on the 737-800 N33289 (measured 737-800 shape): the 2010 gold line is NOT the 2019 navy line's path. It runs level from the nose tip along the forward door's sill, stays level under the wing, crosses the aft door at its sill and rises into the tail cone (in fuselage y it dives toward the belly where the belly sweeps up). 'UNITED' is smaller than first drawn and sits clear above the windows, x 0.20-0.36, cap height about 0.6 m. On the 737-9 MAX (N37504) the line starts higher on the nose and the title starts closer to the door: one type-independent drawing can't fit both.",
  "history": "Added alongside the 2019 standard (Skyarium v1 had only an approximation). Introduced in 2010–2011 after the United–Continental merger, replacing United's 2004 blue scheme and Continental's 1991 one; superseded by the 2019 livery but still flown on part of the fleet."
